{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JrS5y4e38VeydQbQCnMZwT","type":"tool_result","content":"695\t/// unchanged (mirrors C where `*ret` is only written on failure).\n696\t///\n697\t/// C: `picoquic/sockloop.c:picoquic_open_network_wake_up` (line 1705–1725).\n698\tfn open_network_wake_up(thread_ctx: &mut NetworkThreadCtx, _ret: &mut i32) {\n699\t    thread_ctx.wake_up_defined = false;\n700\t    let (sender, receiver) = std::sync::mpsc::channel();\n701\t    thread_ctx.wake_up_sender = Some(sender);\n702\t    thread_ctx.wake_up_receiver = Some(receiver);\n703\t    thread_ctx.wake_up_defined = true;\n704\t}\n705\t\n706\t/// Populate a `pollfd` array for the poll-based packet loop.\n707\t/// Slot 0 holds the wake-up read end (when `thread_ctx.wake_up_defined`);\n708\t/// subsequent slots hold the open socket fds; unused trailing slots are set\n709\t/// to `-1` so `poll(2)` ignores them.\n710\t///\n711\t/// In the Rust translation the internal loop uses `mpsc` channels rather\n712\t/// than `poll(2)`, so `thread_ctx.wake_up_pipe_fd[0]` is always `-1`.\n713\t/// Callers that drive their own `poll(2)` loop should use a real pipe\n714\t/// and set `wake_up_pipe_fd[0]` accordingly."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"883038f3-dc47-41ca-bc79-c1df5243c1d6","uuid":"7b4b1cc2-10f6-4879-838c-3367c2e06d49","timestamp":"2026-05-07T09:31:06.308Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/packet_loop.rs","content":"/// unchanged (mirrors C where `*ret` is only written on failure).\n///\n/// C: `picoquic/sockloop.c:picoquic_open_network_wake_up` (line 1705–1725).\nfn open_network_wake_up(thread_ctx: &mut NetworkThreadCtx, _ret: &mut i32) {\n    thread_ctx.wake_up_defined = false;\n    let (sender, receiver) = std::sync::mpsc::channel();\n    thread_ctx.wake_up_sender = Some(sender);\n    thread_ctx.wake_up_receiver = Some(receiver);\n    thread_ctx.wake_up_defined = true;\n}\n\n/// Populate a `pollfd` array for the poll-based packet loop.\n/// Slot 0 holds the wake-up read end (when `thread_ctx.wake_up_defined`);\n/// subsequent slots hold the open socket fds; unused trailing slots are set\n/// to `-1` so `poll(2)` ignores them.\n///\n/// In the Rust translation the internal loop uses `mpsc` channels rather\n/// than `poll(2)`, so `thread_ctx.wake_up_pipe_fd[0]` is always `-1`.\n/// Callers that drive their own `poll(2)` loop should use a real pipe\n/// and set `wake_up_pipe_fd[0]` accordingly.","numLines":20,"startLine":695,"totalLines":1604}}}
